      "source_example_id": "e-7409",
      "knowledge_point": "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates",
      "kp_id": "e-7409"
    },
    "q-85204": {
      "dom_id": "step-q85204",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14133254/assets/928fb72015b78436f95895799c2846d1a0b34242b7216d31a6ed6bdda46728a1.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{6x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14133254/assets/375e270ce444d9f62b50f9a25abfae781113fdad895833d693061ffffe93f5d3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{6x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{6r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [r]_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (4-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -12\\sqrt{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -12\\sqrt{2}\\,\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[(24\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-12\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))-0] \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}-3\\pi \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
